 using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $22$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $64$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{3{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{1/3}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}{u}^{-1/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{3}{2}\\,{u}^{2/3}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{64}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x=8.\n$$" 13194139535579 true] [17592186355941 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355942 13194139535579 true] [17592186355941 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535579 true] [17592186355941 1041 "field-1" 13194139535579 true] [17592186355941 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355942 13194139535579 true] [17592186355941 1110 #uuid "d6d60f24-43b0-54af-ae32-ca1142305e07" 13194139535579 true] [17592186355942 1034 "8" 13194139535579 